{%- comment -%}
  Keeps the theme's own card geometry out of Appstle's customer portal.

  On /apps/subscriptions Shopify renders Appstle's portal inside this theme's layout, so the
  compiled theme bundle applies to Appstle's markup. Appstle ships that markup with the bare
  class name `card`, and `blocks/_card.liquid` claims the same bare selector in the shared
  bundle. The product-card geometry therefore lands on the portal's subscription cards:
  `display: flex`, `flex-direction: column`, `width: 100%`, `height: 100%`, `overflow: hidden`
  and `container-type: inline-size`.

  Measured on live 2026-10-26: the theme rule does win there. On a portal with no subscriptions
  the card holds one line and nothing is lost, but `height: 100%` with `overflow: hidden` is the
  shape that clips a populated subscription card, and `container-type` changes how any container
  query inside it resolves. Give Appstle's cards their intrinsic box back.

  `position` and the border-* declarations are deliberately left alone. They do not clip, and
  reverting `position: relative` could pull the containing block out from under an absolutely
  positioned child we cannot see from an empty portal.

  Usage, from layout/theme.liquid only:
    {% render 'appstle-portal-styles' %}
{%- endcomment -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  .appstle-customer-portal .card,
  .appstle-wrapper .card {
    display: block;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    overflow: visible;
    container-type: normal;
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
